    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, our pursuit of stolen bases and efficient production centers on **Brice Turang** (70.37) and **Corbin Carroll** (79.87). **Corbin Carroll** remains our premier start; he benefits significantly from a soft matchup against Wilkinson, whose 6.01 xERA is the most exploitable on the board. The added benefit of winds blowing out further inflates his value. **Brice Turang** earns high marks for a different reason: his combination of a lead-off spot and a favorable matchup against Quantrill\u2014who allows for high stolen base volume\u2014makes him a critical engine for our Roto points today. **Seiya Suzuki** also warrants attention with a robust 73.90, as he effectively weaponizes the platoon advantage against Abbott to drive his production profile.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Griffin Conine**, who posted a 43.60 score. While he held steady with positive park factors and a strong lineup slot, the elite pitching profile of Cavalli capped his ceiling, ultimately relegating him to the bench behind the more favorable matchup profiles of **Jose Caballero** and **Mickey Moniak**. We are also closely monitoring **Gabriel Moreno**, whose score of 66.03 is bolstered by the weak 6.01 xERA of Wilkinson; if the wind gusts remain consistent throughout the game, his efficiency could climb even higher.\n\nRegarding risks, we are operating with a clear focus on maximizing our categorical output while protecting our game caps. While we have depth in the utility spot with **Josh Naylor** (66.82), we remain cautious of the volatility inherent in our lower-floor starters like **Cole Young**, who faces a stiffer test against Soriano. As a reminder, **James Wood**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, and **Vinnie Pasquantino** remain unavailable due to IL designations or rest, further concentrating our offensive reliance on our current active starters. We will continue to evaluate the performance of **Henry Bolte** and **Max Muncy** against Baz to ensure we aren't leaving production on the table, but as of this morning, the optimization model holds firm.",
